Read each connector's DDC brightness value once per shell session instead of
on every monitor/bus change.

Noctalia re-runs `ddcutil detect` whenever the monitor list changes, and each
detected DDC monitor then ran `ddcutil getvcp 10` again from initBrightness().
The onBusNumChanged / onIsDdcChanged handlers below fire on exactly the events
that a display hotplug produces, so a connector that flaps costs one extra I2C
transaction per flap.

That matters on this machine because the YCT panel's internal USB hub
re-enumerates on its own every ~10s once it starts (kernel marks it
USB_QUIRK_HUB_SLOW_RESET), taking the DP hotplug line down with it. niri
re-adds DP-1 each cycle, busNum/isDdc change, and Noctalia answered every one
of those with another DDC read on the USB-C connector that was already failing
link training.

Keep DDC brightness control working -- it is the only way to dim this panel --
but stop the per-hotplug re-read. ddcBrightnessByConnector already caches the
value for the connector, so initBrightness() returns early when it is present;
setvcp still runs on demand when the user changes brightness.

Measured before this change: 2792 hub re-enumerations in one boot. Running
ddcutil by hand does not reset the hub by itself (devnum stayed constant across
30 detect/getvcp/setvcp calls), so this patch removes a per-flap cost rather
than claiming DDC is the sole cause.

